Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||06-25-2001||09:15 PM||||Ok well, I had my car up on ramps in the back and noticed that coolant was leaking bad out of the bottom of the radiator!  I checked the coolant tubes going in the radiator and they weren't leaking. (from what I saw)  So what do you guys think is going on?  I sure hope my radiator isnt shot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> im broke.<P>PS it leaks even when the car isn't up on ramps<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||06-25-2001||10:12 PM||||so it is leaking from the frt of the vehicle and it is not the tubs or the hoses it must be the radiator.  You could try radiator stop leak pellets or fluid if you wanted to postpone your repair.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bluebottlejunky||06-25-2001||10:24 PM||||hmmm, werid..but the clamp that holds the tube onto the coolant resivor is gone, i wonder if that is the cause?  But I really dont think that the coolant would work its way all the way down to the bottom of the radiator.<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||4
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-26-2001||08:49 PM||||If you have a VENTED radiator cap that AutoZone and PepBoys calls for in their books then you could be leaking through the overflow bottle.  A vented cap will let coolant siphon through the bottle when the car sits with the nose slightly down.<P>Our cars need a NON-VENTED cap.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bluebottlejunky||06-26-2001||09:22 PM||||hmmm, how can I tell if my radiator cap is vented?<P>------------------<BR>Hello, My name is Mike and I am addicted too fiero's...PLEASE dont help me.<BR>85'GT 4sp<BR>AIM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||5
Z||000005||c m west||06-26-2001||10:06 PM||||A new radiator from Martin Cadillac ( our parts supplier ) is $240.50, factory replacement, where as our local radiator shop charges $240.00 to fix as well as modify it to a brass, 3 core unit - much, much more effiecient. The brass is nice because it can always be fixed<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-26-2001||11:07 PM||||Well... considering GM doesn't make radiators, I don't think I'd ever resort to a dealer.<P>Modine is the line I've used for most things. You can get them thru a bunch of parts stores. Significantly less than a dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WMEBERT||06-27-2001||04:40 PM||||I just put a brand new "Modine" radiator in my '86 GT.  The construction was the same as stock-plastic and aluminum which is fine but I woulda' prefered all brass.  I got it at Advance Auto Parts for $161.99 and a Lifetime Warranty.  Car runs nice and cool now!!  Check ebay, their is a place selling them for $99.00 eith free (I think) delivery.<P>Bill||||reg||3
