Z||000009||Fierowrecker||06-27-2001||06:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bluebottlejunky:<BR><B>hmm, my budjet is lookin kinda low crap me gunn have to wait!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Take a small can of pepper and put it into the engine coolant...<BR>It should seal any SMALL pinholes...<BR>Flush the system when you can replace the radiator...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000010||beachbomb||06-27-2001||06:54 PM||||Bluebottle, i bought a new radiator from a local shop here in jax for about $100, took about 2 hours to replace, not to bad of a job, mine was leaking but i couldn't find exactly where it was coming from, once the radiator was out there was a small split type seam down one side of the radiator...good luck..Jim(2'84 Indys)||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||06-27-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Bluebottlejunky:<BR><B>hmmm, how can I tell if my radiator cap is vented?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remove the cap.  There's a little metal disc on the underside (size of a dime ?).  Pull the disc gently and let it snap back.  The disc <B>should</B> be lightly spring-loaded to the bottom of the cap.  <P>If the disc falls away from the bottom of the cap by its own weight, you have a vented (or a defective) cap.  Not what you want.<BR>  <P> ||||reg||1
